PRC No. 10252–1926 Filed 7–1–21; 8:45 am

BILLING CODE 4301-41-P

DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR

Bureau of Indian Affairs

Bureau of Indian Affairs

Termination of Preparation of the Environmental Impact Statement for the Farming and Manoe-Gallup Removal Management Plan Amendment, New Mexico

AGENCY: Bureau of Land Management; Bureau of Indian Affairs, Interior.
ACTION: Notice of termination.
SUMMARY: The Bureau of Land Management (BLM) and the Bureau of Indian Affairs (BIA) are terminating the preparation of an environmental impact statement (ES) for the Farming Manage-Callup Removal Management Plan (RMP) Amendment.
DATES: The EIS development process for the Farming and Manoe-Gallup Removal Management Plan (RMP) and the United States (U.S.) Farming and Manoe-Gallup Removal Management Plan (RMP).
FOR FURTHER INFORMATION CONTACT: BLM Farming and Manoe-Gallup Removal Management Plan, Scott, Scott & Scott Group 505–564–7689 or BIA Navajo Region Officer Regional Archeological/Project Manager Robert Begov, robert.begov@18.gov for 2005–063–8515. Individuals in the United States who are dead, deshland, hard of hearing, or have a speech disability may also be�or-like to the National Environmental Policy Act of 1963, as implemented by the Council on Environmental Quality regulations, the BLM announced its intention to prepare an RMA and implement and associated EIS on February 25, 2014 (79 FR 10548). On October 21, 2016, the RMA has been issued the BLA, and the RMA amended Notice of Interim in the Federal Register amending the addition of the BLA as a joint-co-lead agency for the EIS (81 FR 2919). The RMA has been used to analyze the impacts of additional oil and gas development within the San Juan Basin in northwestern New Mexico.

Mexico, as well as decisions related to lands and reality, BLM-managed lands were required to provide a characteristic, and vegetation management. The EIS was also to evaluate alternatives and issues related to the BLA. The BLA is a general and non-national enforcement activity. The Federal State and local lawsuits are on Navajo Tribal Trust Lands and Navajo Indian Islands. The Notice of availability for the Draft EIS published in the Federal Register on February 28, 2020 (85 FR 12012). The basis for the BLA is also available to various Federal, State, and local agencies, elected officials, special groups, and other individuals, and the media. Due to the COVID-19 pandemic and restrictions placed on in-person meetings, virtual public hearings (up to 100 min) have been held for the 2020, 2021, 2022, and 2023. Since the initial publication of the Notice of Interim in 2014 and 2016, the publication of the draft RMP Amendment and EIS in 2020, there have left many changes relevant to the plan amendment and associated EIS, such as a change in the development trends in the San Juan Basin, the withdrawal of 2004 acres from mineral entry around the Chaco Culture National Historical Park; the preparation of BLA for the State of California; the distribution of the information for the region; the establishment of the Honoring Chaco Initiative; and an increase in outdoor recreation in the region. Given these changes and the extent of revisions necessary to address these changes in the current EIS, EMS are subject to the impact of the plan amendment to continue the plan amendment effort as currently structured. Therefore, the BLM and BLA hereby terminate the preparation of the EIS for the RMP Amendment.

(Authority: 40 CFR 1056.4, 40 CFR 1061.10)

Melanie G. Barnes.

BLM New Mexico State Director.
Deborah S. Ashley.

Acting BIA Navajo Region Director.
